B. Database Schema

User

{ name, email, password (hashed), role: 'admin', createdAt }

Only one admin user is expected, but keep role on the schema now — costs nothing and means you don't need a migration if you ever add staff logins.

Category

{ name, slug, createdAt }

Brand

{ name, slug, logo (optional), createdAt }

Product

{
  name, slug, description, price,
  category: ObjectId → Category,
  brand: ObjectId → Brand,
  images: [ { url, publicId } ],
  sizes: [String],           // e.g. ["S","M","L","XL"]
  colors: [String],
  stock: Number,
  featured: Boolean,
  isNew: Boolean,
  createdAt, updatedAt
}

Settings (a single document — no admin needs more than one shop record)

{
  shopName, aboutText,
  phone, whatsappNumber, address, mapEmbedUrl,
  openingHours, instagramUrl, facebookUrl,
  paymentQr: { url, publicId }
}

Relationships: Product → Category (many-to-one), Product → Brand (many-to-one). Category and Brand are referenced by ObjectId and populated on read — this is what lets "add a category from the admin panel" work without touching Product code.

Future (not built now): Order { user?, items: [{product, qty, size, color, priceAtPurchase}], total, status, paymentProof }, Payment { order, method, status, transactionRef }. Because cart items already carry {productId, qty, size, color} (see section H), turning a cart into an Order later is a straight mapping, not a redesign.

