D. Phase-by-Phase Roadmap

Each phase below is intentionally compact — when you're ready to actually build a phase, ask for it by number and I'll walk you through it step by step with real code, one file at a time, as you asked.

Phase 1 — Requirements & Planning

Build: Finalize feature list (done above), confirm hosting choices, register/confirm domain if any. Why: Prevents scope creep once code starts. Test/Done when: You can describe the full v1 feature set in one paragraph without hesitation.

Phase 2 — Project Setup

Build: server/ with Express + nodemon + dotenv; client/ with Vite + React + Tailwind; git repo with .gitignore for both. Files: server.js, package.json ×2, .env ×2, vite.config.js, tailwind.config.js. Concepts: monorepo vs. two-repo layout, environment variables, CORS. Done when: npm run dev on backend logs "Server running," Vite dev server shows the default React page, and a basic CORS-enabled GET /api/health returns { status: 'ok' }.

Phase 3 — Database Design

Build: Create the four Mongoose schemas (User, Product, Category, Brand) + Settings. Files: models/*.js. Concepts: Mongoose schemas, refs/population, indexes on slug. Done when: You can mongoose.connect to Atlas and see empty collections created for each model.

Phase 4 — Backend Setup

Build: config/db.js, centralized error middleware, base Express app structure. Files: config/db.js, middleware/errorMiddleware.js, server.js. Concepts: middleware order, async error handling (express-async-handler or a try/catch wrapper). Done when: A deliberately-thrown error in a dummy route returns a clean JSON error instead of crashing the server.

Phase 5 — Authentication

Build: Admin registration (one-time seed script, not a public route), login, JWT issuing, authMiddleware. Files: models/User.js, controllers/authController.js, routes/authRoutes.js, middleware/authMiddleware.js, utils/generateToken.js. APIs: POST /auth/login, GET /auth/me. Concepts: bcrypt hashing, JWT sign/verify, protecting routes. Done when: Wrong password is rejected, correct login returns a token, and hitting a protected test route without a token returns 401.

Phase 6 — Product API

Build: Full CRUD, plus apiFeatures.js for search/filter/sort/pagination. Files: models/Product.js, controllers/productController.js, routes/productRoutes.js, utils/apiFeatures.js. Done when: Postman/Thunder Client can create, list (with filters), update, and delete a product.

Phase 7 — Category & Brand APIs

Build: CRUD for both, plus "products in category" endpoint. Files: matching models/, controllers/, routes/ for each. Done when: A product can be created referencing a real category/brand _id, and deleting a category you decide to prevent if products still reference it (or cascade — decide now, document the choice).

Phase 8 — Cloudinary Image Upload

Build: Multer memory storage → stream to Cloudinary → store {url, publicId} on Product/Settings. Files: config/cloudinary.js, middleware/uploadMiddleware.js, utils/cloudinaryUpload.js. Done when: Uploading an image via Postman returns a working Cloudinary URL, and deleting a product also deletes its Cloudinary images (no orphaned files).

Phase 9 — Admin Dashboard (Frontend)

Build: Admin login page, protected admin layout, product/category/brand management tables + forms, settings/QR editor. Files: pages/admin/*, components/admin/*, context/AuthContext.jsx, components/common/ProtectedRoute.jsx. Done when: You can log in as admin, add a product with images, and see it appear in the list — entirely through the UI, no Postman.

Phase 10 — Customer Frontend Shell

Build: Home, Shop, layout (Navbar/Footer), routing. Files: pages/customer/Home.jsx, pages/customer/Shop.jsx, components/layout/*, routes/AppRoutes.jsx. Done when: Home shows featured/new-arrival products fetched from the real API; Shop shows the full grid.

Phase 11 — Search / Filter / Sort

Build: Wire the filter UI to the GET /products query params built in Phase 6. Files: components/filters/*. Done when: Combining category + price range + a search term returns correct, server-filtered results (not client-side filtering of a full list).

Phase 12 — Product Details Page

Build: Image gallery, size/color selectors, stock status, WhatsApp button. Files: pages/customer/ProductDetails.jsx, components/product/ProductGallery.jsx. Done when: Selecting a size/color and clicking WhatsApp opens a pre-filled WhatsApp chat with the product name.

Phase 13 — Cart (localStorage)

Build: CartContext, add/remove/update-quantity, persisted to localStorage. Files: context/CartContext.jsx, hooks/useCart.js, components/cart/*, pages/customer/Cart.jsx. Done when: Cart survives a page refresh, quantities and totals update correctly, and out-of-stock products can't be added.

Phase 14 — WhatsApp / Contact

Build: Contact page pulling from Settings (phone, WhatsApp, map embed, hours, social links). Files: pages/customer/Contact.jsx. Done when: Every contact detail on the page is admin-editable, not hardcoded.

Phase 15 — QR Payment Display

Build: Payment QR shown on Cart/Contact page, sourced from Settings. Done when: Changing the QR in the admin panel instantly reflects on the customer side (after refresh).

Phase 16 — Validation & Error Handling

Build: Server-side validation (required fields, price > 0, valid ObjectId refs) + friendly frontend error messages/toasts. Files: middleware/validateMiddleware.js, error boundaries/toasts on frontend. Done when: Submitting a bad product form (backend or frontend-bypassed) never crashes the app or silently fails.

Phase 17 — Responsive Design

Build: Mobile-first pass over every page with Tailwind breakpoints. Done when: Home, Shop, Product Details, Cart, Contact, and the admin panel are all usable at 375px width.

Phase 18 — Testing

Build: Manual test pass using the checklists below; optionally a few Jest/Supertest tests on critical backend routes (auth, product CRUD). Done when: Every item in the Testing Checklist (section L) passes.

Phase 19 — Deployment

Build: Deploy backend, frontend, connect to Atlas, set production env vars, connect custom domain if applicable. Done when: The live URL works end-to-end: a real visitor can browse and add to cart, and you can log in and manage products from the deployed admin panel.

Phase 20 — Maintenance & Future Upgrades

Build: Nothing yet — this phase is where Orders, eSewa/Khalti, accounts, reviews, wishlist, Socket.IO chat get layered in later, following the same model→controller→route→api→component pattern used throughout.

E. Admin Workflow (Login → Product → Live on Site)
Admin visits /admin/login, submits credentials → POST /auth/login → JWT stored (in memory/context, not localStorage, to reduce XSS exposure — or httpOnly cookie if you want to go further later).
Token attached via Axios interceptor to all subsequent admin requests (Authorization: Bearer <token>).
Admin opens "Add Product," fills form, selects category/brand from dropdowns (populated via GET /categories, GET /brands), uploads images.
Frontend sends multipart/form-data → POST /products → authMiddleware verifies token → uploadMiddleware sends images to Cloudinary → controller saves Product doc with {url, publicId} image references.
Product now exists in MongoDB — the very next GET /products call from any customer browser returns it. No caching/rebuild step needed since data is fetched live.
F. Customer Workflow (Home → Shop → Product → Cart → Contact)
Home loads → GET /products?featured=true and GET /products?isNew=true populate the two carousels.
Customer clicks "Shop" → Shop page loads all products, applies any filters via query params.
Customer clicks a product → GET /products/:slug loads details.
Customer picks size/color, clicks "Add to Cart" → item pushed into CartContext state → context effect writes the whole cart array to localStorage.
Customer opens Cart → reads directly from context (already hydrated from localStorage on app load) → sees line items, subtotal, total.
Customer clicks WhatsApp (on product page or cart) → opens https://wa.me/<number>?text=<prefilled message>.
G. Authentication Flow (JWT, explained plainly)
On login, the server checks the email/password against the hashed password in MongoDB (bcrypt.compare).
If valid, it signs a JWT: a token containing { id, role }, a secret key, and an expiry (e.g. 7 days). The token is not stored server-side — that's the whole point of JWT, it's stateless.
The client stores this token and sends it back on every admin request in the Authorization header.
authMiddleware.verifyToken decodes and verifies the signature on each protected request. If valid, it attaches req.user and calls next(). If invalid/expired, it returns 401 before the controller ever runs.
A second middleware, requireAdmin, checks req.user.role === 'admin' — separating "is this a real logged-in user" from "is this user allowed to do this," which matters once you add customer accounts later.
H. Cloudinary Upload Flow
Frontend sends a normal multipart/form-data request (via a <input type="file" multiple> and FormData).
multer (configured with memoryStorage, not disk storage) parses the incoming files into req.files as buffers — nothing touches your server's disk.
cloudinaryUpload.js streams each buffer to Cloudinary's upload API using cloudinary.uploader.upload_stream.
Cloudinary returns a secure_url and public_id for each image — these two fields are what you save on the Product document (never save the raw file).
On product delete/edit-removing-an-image, call cloudinary.uploader.destroy(publicId) so you don't accumulate orphaned images in your Cloudinary account.
I. Cart Architecture (localStorage → future Orders)
Cart shape: [{ productId, name, price, image, size, color, qty }] — deliberately mirrors what an eventual Order line item needs.
CartContext holds this array in React state; a useEffect syncs it to localStorage on every change; on app mount, another effect hydrates state from localStorage.
When Orders are added later: "Checkout" simply POSTs the current cart array (plus customer/shipping info) to POST /orders, the backend re-validates price/stock server-side (never trust the client's stored price), creates the Order, and the frontend clears the cart. Nothing about the Cart's internal shape needs to change.
J. Where State Lives — React State vs Context vs localStorage (no Redux)
Local component state (useState): form inputs, modal open/close, per-page filter UI before it's applied — anything only that component cares about.
Context (CartContext, AuthContext): state needed across unrelated parts of the tree — cart badge in the Navbar and the Cart page and the Product page all need cart state; login status is needed by the Navbar, ProtectedRoute, and every admin page.
localStorage: only the cart array and (optionally) the JWT if you choose to persist admin login across refreshes. Nothing else — product/category/brand data always comes fresh from the API, never cached in localStorage, or the admin's edits won't show up for customers.
Redux is not needed here. Two contexts cover every cross-cutting state need in this app. Reach for Redux only if state logic gets genuinely complex (many interdependent slices, frequent cross-slice updates) — this project won't hit that bar even with Orders added later.
K. Frontend vs Backend Responsibility
Concern	Frontend	Backend
Displaying products	✅	—
Filtering/search/sort logic	sends query params	✅ executes the query
Price calculation (cart subtotal)	✅ (display only)	—
Price validation (once Orders exist)	—	✅ always re-check server-side
Stock enforcement	✅ (disable "Add to Cart" UI)	✅ (source of truth — never trust frontend alone)
Auth token storage	✅	issues + verifies tokens
Image upload	✅ (file picker, FormData)	✅ (Cloudinary streaming, saving URLs)
Input validation	✅ (UX — instant feedback)	✅ (security — never skip, frontend checks are bypassable)
